# campus-fix v2.9: aggregate LAN SYN rate cap (fw4 nftables drop-in)
#
# Single-rule greylist increment (v2.7.0 incident policy: one rule at a time;
# fw4guard boot dry-run quarantines ALL drop-ins on failure, so the worst
# case is "this rule silently off", never a dead LAN).
#
# Mechanism: N devices behind one NAT IP produce an aggregate new-connection
# (SYN) rate that a single host rarely reaches - bursty page loads from
# several devices overlap, and campus flow profilers use exactly that burst
# shape as a "proxy behavior" signal. This caps the AGGREGATE rate of
# LAN-originated SYNs to a single-host-plausible envelope.
#
# Why stateless `limit` and NOT a meter / ct-count-in-set:
#   - `limit` is per-rule global: one token bucket for the whole bridge,
#     which is exactly the post-NAT traffic shape the campus sees (per-host
#     meters would cap each LAN client separately and leave the aggregate
#     untouched)
#   - the limit expression is ancient core nft with universal kernel
#     support; v2.7.0's ct-count-in-set passed CI `nft -c` but was rejected
#     by the target kernel (CI cannot catch kernel-semantic rejection)
#
# Thresholds: 40 SYN/s sustained, 100-packet burst allowance. A single busy
# browser bursts ~30-60 SYNs on a heavy page load; the burst bucket absorbs
# that untouched. Sustained rates above 40/s only happen with parallel
# downloaders / multi-device storms. Excess SYNs are dropped and TCP
# retransmits after ~1s, so exceeding the cap degrades to mild latency,
# never a broken connection.
#
# Deliberately NOT in this increment (rejected):
#   - per-host meters (ip saddr key): campus only sees the aggregate
#   - new-flow-per-minute ct counters: kernel-support risk (v2.7.0)
#   - TCP window-size / option-order rewriting: not expressible in nftables

chain campus_synrate {
	type filter hook forward priority filter - 8; policy accept;
	iifname "br-lan" tcp flags & (syn | ack) == syn limit rate over 40/second burst 100 packets counter drop comment "campus-fix: aggregate LAN SYN rate cap (smooth multi-device burst signature)"
}
